          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "The first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure typically includes the initiation of drug treatment alongside lifestyle changes. For most adults without other complications, the first-line drug classes include:\n\n1. Thiazide-type diuretics\n2. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\n3. Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)\n4. Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers\n\nIt's important to note that ACE inhibitors and ARBs should not be combined due to the risk of harm without added benefit. For patients with stage 1 hypertension who have conditions like cardiovascular disease, diabetes, or chronic kidney disease, drug treatment should begin immediately. If these conditions are not present, lifestyle measures should be the first approach, with reassessment after three to six months before considering medication. This information comes from the \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\" guideline.\n\nI could not cover specific medication names or dosages, as that information is beyond the guidelines provided.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
